ed}P(\\overset{―}{X}>105) & =1-Φ(1.05) \\\\  & =1-0.8531 \\\\  & =0.1469.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534439 17592186045417 true] [17592186162386 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534439 17592186045417 true] [17592186162386 1071 "Given that $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{36}∼N(0, 18^{2})$ is a random sample of size $36$ from a normal population, calculate $P(\\mid X^(―) \\mid > 0.8)$.\n\n*Hint: A value table of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.06$ | $0.07$ | $0.08$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.2$ | $0.6026$ | $0.6064$ | $0.6103$ |\n| $0.3$ | $0.6406$ | $0.6443$ | $0.6480$ |\n| $0.4$ | $0.6772$ | $0.6808$ | $0.6844$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139534439 17592186045417 false] [17592186162386 1071 "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{36}∼N(0,{18}^{2})$ is a random sample of size $36$ from a normal population, calculate $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.8).$\n\n*Hint: A value table of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.6026\\,$ | $\\,0.6064\\,$ | $\\,0.6103\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6406\\,$ | $\\,0.6443\\,$ | $\\,0.6480\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6772\\,$ | $\\,0.6808\\,$ | $\\,0.6844\\,$ |" 13194139534439 17592186045417 true] [17592186162386 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a normal population, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ has the distribution\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(0,\\frac{{18}^{2}}{36}) \\\\  & ∼N(0,9).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're required to find $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.8).$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.8) & =1-P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<0.8) \\\\  & =1-P(-0.8<\\overset{―}{X}<0.8) \\\\  & =1-P(\\frac{-0.8-0}{\\sqrt{9}}<Z<\\frac{0.8-0}{\\sqrt{9}}) \\\\  & =1-P(-0.27<Z<0.27) \\\\  & =1-(Φ(0.27)-Φ(-0.27)) \\\\  & =1+Φ(-0.27)-Φ(0.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.27) & =0.6064,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by the symmetry of the normal distribution,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.27) & =1-Φ(0.27) \\\\  & =1-0.6064 \\\\  & =0.3936.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>0.8) & =1+Φ(-0.27)-Φ(0.27) \\\\  & =1+0.3936-0.6064 \\\\  & =0.7872.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534439 17592186045417 true] [17592186163327 1064 "Let $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ be a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a normal population $N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2$. In other words,\n\n$$\nX_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n} \\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can easily determine the sampling distribution of the sample mean\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- The sum of $n$ independent and identically distributed normal random variables is normally distributed. Thus,\n$Y = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n X_i$\nis normally distributed.\n- A normally distributed random variable scaled by a constant factor is normally distributed. Thus,\n$\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\cdot Y$\nis normally distributed.\n\nAlso, we previously saw that the expected value of the sample mean equals the population mean, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu\n$$\n\nand the variance of the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$\n\nPutting all of this together, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \\sim N\\left(\\mu, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right)\n$$\n\nIn other words, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu$ and variance\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\n$$" 13194139534439 17592186045417 false] [17592186163327 1064 "Let $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ be a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a normal population $N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)$ with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2.$ In other words,   \n\n$$\nX_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n} \\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2).\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can easily determine the sampling distribution of the sample mean\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n X_i.\n$$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- The sum of $n$ independent and identically distributed normal random variables is normally distributed. Thus, \n\n$$\nY = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nis normally distributed.\n\n- A normally distributed random variable scaled by a constant factor is normally distributed. Thus, \n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\cdot Y\n$$\n\nis normally distributed.\n\nAlso, we previously saw that the expected value of the sample mean equals the population mean, i.e., \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu,\n$$\n\nand the variance of the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[\\overline{X}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}.\n$$\n\nPutting all of this together, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} \\sim N\\left(\\mu, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right),\n$$\n\nIn other words, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{n}.$" 13194139534439 17592186045417 true] [17592186421562 1080 "N(6,2)" 13194139534439 17592186045417 true] [17592186421562 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534439 17592186045417 true] [17592186421562 1083 #uuid "04699e55-d487-568d-acba-2efd6750322d" 13194139534439 17592186045417 true] [17592186421563 1080 "0.0239" 13194139534439 17592186045417 true] [17592186421563 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534439 17592186045417 true] [17592186421563 1083 #uuid "13b2844b-b946-5f0d-9374-6c50